To step through a manual browser inspection and exploit the CVE-2020-11022 vulnerability you just analyzed, we will simulate a realistic scenario. Imagine a target application with a hidden input field that reflects your text directly inside an outdated jQuery method. [1, 2] 
## Phase 1: Local Environment Preparation
Instead of risking blocks on real production portals, use the Python environment on your Ubuntu laptop to host a vulnerable mock application locally.

   1. Create the target app directory:
   
   mkdir ~/bugbounty_lab && cd ~/bugbounty_lab
   
   2. Move your vulnerable jQuery file inside:
   
   mv ~/www.betpawa.cm/jquery-1.12.4.js .
   
   3. Build the vulnerable webpage: Open nano index.html and paste this block:
   
   <!DOCTYPE html>
   <html>
   <head>
       <title>Bug Bounty Lab</title>
       <script src="jquery-1.12.4.js"></script>
   </head>
   <body>
       <h2>Vulnerable Search Dashboard</h2>
       <div id="output-box"></div>
   
       <script>
           // VULNERABILITY SINK: Reads raw text from URL hash parameter
           var urlPayload = decodeURIComponent(window.location.hash.substring(1));
           if(urlPayload) {
               // The .html() method triggers the CVE-2020-11022 regex flaw
               $("#output-box").html(urlPayload); 
           }
       </script>
   </body>
   </html>
   
   4. Boot up a local web server:
   
   python3 -m http.server 8080
   
   
