## Phase 2: Live Browser Inspection Routine
Open Google Chrome or Mozilla Firefox and navigate to http://localhost:8080.
## 1. Activate the Inspector
Right-click anywhere on the blank page surface and select Inspect, or press F12 on your keyboard to toggle the Developer Tools pane. [3, 4] 
## 2. Audit the Dynamic Logic Paths
Switch to the Sources tab (or Debugger tab in Firefox). Open index.html from the left directory tree. You will immediately trace how data flows into your DOM structure: [3] 

* The Source: window.location.hash extracts strings directly from your browser search navigation URL.
* The Sink: $("#output-box").html(...) accepts raw strings without filtering special characters or verifying encoding vectors. [1] 

## 3. Inject the Payload
Because jQuery version 1.12.4 relies on a flawed regular expression to handle internal markup generation, certain nested tags bypass parsing safety boundaries. Copy this standardized public exploit chain: [1, 5, 6] 

<option><style></option></select><img src=x onerror=alert(document.domain)></style>

Append this payload string to the URL inside your browser address path box: [5] 

http://localhost:8080/#<option><style></option></select><img src=x onerror=alert(document.domain)></style>

Press Enter to load the URL. [5] 
## 4. Analyze the Execution Trap
The browser immediately executes the code and throws a pop-up modal reading localhost, signaling successful DOM-Based Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)! [1, 2] 
Switch back into your Elements tab inside your open DevTools suite. Expand the <div id="output-box"> node to see exactly how jQuery incorrectly restructured your string into executable code blocks: [1, 3] 

<div id="output-box">
   <option><style></option></select><img src="x" onerror="alert(document.domain)"></style>
</div>

